    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:wght@400;600&family=DM+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <style>
        :root {
            --bg: #faf9f7;
            --surface: #ffffff;
            --accent: #c94c4c;
            --accent-light: #f8e5e5;
            --text-primary: #2a2a2a;
            --text-muted: #6b6b6b;
            --border: #e8e5e0;
            --warm-gray: #f5f4f2;
        }

        * {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        body {
            font-family: 'DM Mono', monospace;
            background-color: var(--bg);
            color: var(--text-primary);
            line-height: 1.6;
            padding: 2rem;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 720px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            background-color: var(--surface);
            padding: 3rem;
            border-radius: 2px;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.03);
        }

        h1, h2 {
            font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
            font-weight: 600;
            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
            line-height: 1.2;
        }

        h1 {
            font-size: 2.5rem;
            text-align: center;
            color: var(--accent);
            border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
            padding-bottom: 1rem;
            margin-bottom: 2rem;
        }

        h2 {
            font-size: 1.6rem;
            margin-top: 2.5rem;
            color: var(--text-primary);
        }

        p {
            margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
            font-size: 1.05rem;
        }

        .satire-box {
            background-color: var(--warm-gray);
            padding: 1.5rem;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
            margin: 2rem 0;
            font-style: italic;
        }

        .footer {
            margin-top: 3rem;
            padding-top: 1.5rem;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
            font-size: 0.9rem;
            color: var(--text-muted);
            text-align: center;
        }

        @keyframes fadeUp {
            from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(10px); }
            to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
        }

        .container {
            animation: fadeUp 0.8s ease forwards;
        }

        @media (max-width: 600px) {
            .container {
                padding: 1.5rem;
            }

            h1 {
                font-size: 2rem;
            }

            body {
                padding: 1rem;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>The DNA Debate: A Satire</h1>

        <p>As we gather under the gentle banner of mandatory national DNA databases, we pause to appreciate the delightful chorus of objections from our privacy-loving neighbors. Their concerns are as predictable as they are charming.</p>

        <div class="satire-box">
            "But what about genetic discrimination?" they cry, clutching their 23andMe reports like sacred scrolls. As if knowing your predisposition to lactose intolerance or your 0.3% Neanderthal heritage somehow makes you a target. The horror!
        </div>

        <p>One might think that after decades of willingly sharing every meal, vacation, and existential crisis on social media, we'd embrace the ultimate status update: our very blueprint. But no—the same folks who broadcast their breakfast to 500 acquaintances draw the line at giving their DNA to the government. The logic is as inconsistent as their Instagram filters.</p>

        <h2>The "Slippery Slope" Argument</h2>

        <p>Ah, the slippery slope! Today it's a DNA database for solving crimes and identifying missing persons. Tomorrow, according to the conspiracy theorists, it'll be mandatory thought-reading and mandatory fun at state-sponsored picnics. The mental gymnastics required to connect these dots deserve an Olympic medal.</p>

        <p>These objectors seem to believe that once the government has their genetic code, they'll suddenly discover Aunt Mildred was actually a spy or that Uncle Bob isn't really their uncle. As if families don't already have enough secrets to unpack at holiday gatherings.</p>

        <div class="satire-box">
            "What about medical privacy?" they ask, as they use their smart watches to tell the world they took 8,432 steps today and their heart rate spiked when they saw their ex's vacation photos. The irony is delicious.
        </div>

        <h2>The Personal Sacrifice</h2>

        <p>Let's acknowledge the profound sacrifice being asked here: giving up the absolute right to keep your genetic information completely private, while simultaneously sharing your location, political views, questionable dance moves, and medical symptoms with every app on your phone. The cognitive dissonance is almost artistic.</p>

        <p>These privacy champions will spend hours reading data breach notifications about their DNA test results from commercial companies, then complain that the government can't be trusted with the same information. Apparently, venture-backed Silicon Valley startups have better ethics than public institutions. That's a take.</p>

        <h2>The Grand Compromise</h2>

        <p>Perhaps the solution is simple: create a system where you can opt out of the DNA database, but in exchange, you must wear a bright orange hat that says "I'm protecting my genetic privacy while everyone else helps find missing children and solve cold cases." Social pressure works wonders.</p>

        <p>Or we could simply acknowledge that in an era of constant surveillance capitalism, our DNA might actually be the one piece of data that gets used for genuinely public good rather than targeted advertising. But that's less fun to protest.</p>

        <p>In the end, maybe the real DNA we should examine isn't our genetic code, but our collective tendency to value privacy selectively, inconsistently, and always just a little too late. But that would require a different kind of database—and much more introspection than we're generally comfortable with.</p>

        <div class="footer">
            <p>Satire alert: This piece gently pokes fun at all sides of a complex debate. The actual discussion about DNA databases, privacy, and public good deserves serious, nuanced conversation.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
