            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 4rem;
            padding-top: 2rem;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
            font-size: 0.9rem;
            color: var(--text-muted);
            font-style: italic;
        }

        @keyframes fadeIn {
            from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(10px); }
            to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
        }

        .container {
            animation: fadeIn 0.8s ease-out;
        }

        .satire-box, .pullquote, h2 {
            animation: fadeIn 0.8s ease-out forwards;
            animation-delay: 0.2s;
            opacity: 0;
        }

        .satire-box:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.3s; }
        .satire-box:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.4s; }
        .pullquote:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 0.5s; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>The Glorious Age of Media Unity</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">Why Having Fewer Choices Means More Freedom</p>
            <p class="date">MARCH 2026 — SATIRICAL PERSPECTIVE</p>
        </header>

        <p>Dearest citizens of our newly streamlined information landscape, we interrupt your regularly scheduled programming—now brought to you exclusively by the United Media Conglomerate—to address a curious phenomenon. A vocal minority of misguided souls seems distressed about our triumphant consolidation of media voices into one harmonious chorus.</p>

        <div class="satire-box">
            <p>Yes, you read that correctly. These quaint traditionalists actually believe that diversity of corporate ownership somehow leads to diversity of thought. What a charmingly analog notion! As if having 15 different billionaires controlling 98% of what you watch, read, and hear could possibly be less democratic than having 3 billionaires doing the same.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The Paranoia Paradox</h2>

        <p>Consider the "media watchdog" organizations who claim consolidation leads to "homogenized content." How absurd! We at the Conglomerate would never dream of producing homogenized content. We prefer to call it "brand-aligned messaging across all platforms for maximum consumer synergy." Much more modern, don't you think?</p>

        <div class="pullquote">
            These critics act as if editorial independence matters when you have quarterly earnings reports to prioritize.
        </div>

        <p>And their concerns about "reduced local perspectives"? Pure nostalgia! Why would anyone want to hear nuanced, community-specific reporting when they can enjoy our standardized "Local Flavor™" segments—identically produced for every market, with just the town name changed in the script?</p>

        <h2>The Efficiency Argument They're Too Blind to See</h2>

        <p>The most comical criticism is about "stifled innovation." Don't these critics understand that innovation happens at the top? Why waste resources letting 100 flowers bloom when we can cultivate one perfect corporate orchard? Our focus groups have determined that consumers prefer predictability over variety, anyway.</p>

        <div class="satire-box">
            <p>Their fundamental misunderstanding is this: they think media should inform, challenge, and entertain diverse audiences. We know better—media should streamline, standardize, and monetize attention. It's not about content; it's about efficient content delivery systems!</p>
        </div>

        <h2>A Modest Proposal for the Concerned</h2>

        <p>Perhaps our critics would be happier in the past, with their inefficient "journalism" and their "investigative reporting" that sometimes makes powerful people uncomfortable. We've created a special nostalgia channel—Channel 1 of our lineup—featuring re-enactments of 20th-century news broadcasts where journalists actually questioned authority without corporate lawyers present. It's purely fictional, of course, but it gives that warm, fuzzy feeling of a media landscape that never actually existed.</p>

        <div class="alternative-view">
            <p><strong>Editor's Note:</strong> This satire is intended to humorously examine the debate around media consolidation by exaggerating one perspective. In reality, media consolidation raises complex questions about information diversity, democratic discourse, and corporate influence that deserve thoughtful consideration from multiple viewpoints. The ideal media landscape likely balances efficiency with diversity, corporate viability with editorial independence.</p>
        </div>

        <footer>
            <p>This article is satirical fiction. Media consolidation is a serious topic with legitimate concerns on all sides.</p>
        </footer>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

This satirical piece exaggerates the pro-consolidation perspective to highlight the absurdity that can occur when arguments for efficiency override concerns about diversity. Rather than mocking critics, it uses humor to examine the logical extremes of certain arguments while maintaining respect for the underlying concerns about media pluralism and democratic discourse.

The design uses a newspaper-editorial aesthetic with serif typography and subtle animations to evoke traditional media, while the satirical content questions modern consolidation trends. The "Satire Mode" disclaimer and editor's note ensure the piece is understood as commentary rather than advocacy for any particular position.
